### Summary

`surrealkv` exports `InternalKeyRef<'a>` at the crate root and a **safe** constructor `InternalKeyRef::from_encoded(&[u8])`. The only length guard is a `debug_assert!(encoded.len() >= 16)` (compiled out in release builds), and every accessor performs unchecked length arithmetic on the slice: `user_key()` slices `&encoded[..len - 16]`, `trailer()`/`seq_num()` compute `let n = len - 16` and pass it to `read_u64_be`, and `timestamp()` computes `let n = len - 8`. `read_u64_be` performs `buffer.as_ptr().add(offset)` followed by an 8-byte typed load. A short slice therefore underflows `usize` in release builds, wraps the pointer to `base - k`, and reads 8 bytes **before** the allocation - an out-of-bounds read (undefined behavior), confirmed with AddressSanitizer (`heap-buffer-overflow`, `READ of size 8`, address exactly `base - 4` for a 4-byte slice). In debug builds the same call panics (`attempt to subtract with overflow`, or the `debug_assert!`), i.e. the only guard is debug-only. No `unsafe` is required at the call site, and `surrealkv` is a published crate, so any downstream Rust code can trigger it. The same ≥16-byte invariant is also absent at the on-disk decode boundary: `BlockIterator::seek_next_entry` reconstructs `current_key` from block bytes with only upper-bound checks, and `BlockIterator::key()` wraps it in `InternalKeyRef` (debug assert only). SurrealDB's `kv-surrealkv` wrapper calls `key_ref.timestamp()` directly on those refs (`surrealdb/core/src/kvs/surrealkv/mod.rs`), so a crafted or corrupted SSTable can reach the same unchecked subtraction in the product that ships this engine.

### Details

**Verified snapshot.** `surrealkv 0.21.2` (crates.io, checksum `b0672cbbe282723a62ccee14b95232ca0b4c9bf44ea22fb520c43e7c517fb8ec`), repository tag `v0.21.2` (commit `d7e85669f59493c9501adcf0289e497ee206ffae`). Consumer verified: `surrealdb/surrealdb` `3.3.0-nightly` (commit `18971ffba781a796c0ad3897bb4b82432c241e4c`) pins `surrealkv = "0.21.2"` at workspace `Cargo.toml:88`, enables it via `surrealdb/core/Cargo.toml:35,76` (`kv-surrealkv`), `surrealdb/server/Cargo.toml:51` (`storage-surrealkv`) and `surrealdb/Cargo.toml:26` (SDK). We verified only this snapshot; earlier/later releases should be checked for the same `from_encoded`/accessor shape, and the maintainers should set the final affected/patched ranges.

**Root cause.** `InternalKeyRef` and its accessors (`surrealkv-0.21.2/src/lib.rs:904-942`):

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
pub struct InternalKeyRef<'a> {
    encoded: &'a [u8],
}

impl<'a> InternalKeyRef<'a> {
    #[inline]
    pub fn from_encoded(encoded: &'a [u8]) -> Self {
        debug_assert!(encoded.len() >= 16);
        Self { encoded }
    }

    #[inline]
    pub fn user_key(&self) -> &'a [u8] { InternalKey::user_key_from_encoded(self.encoded) }

    #[inline]
    pub fn trailer(&self) -> u64 { InternalKey::trailer_from_encoded(self.encoded) }

    #[inline]
    pub fn seq_num(&self) -> u64 { InternalKey::seq_num_from_encoded(self.encoded) }

    #[inline]
    pub fn timestamp(&self) -> u64 {
        let n = self.encoded.len() - 8;
        read_u64_be(self.encoded, n)
    }
}
```

and the helpers it delegates to (`src/lib.rs:696-702,798-828`):

```rust
fn read_u64_be(buffer: &[u8], offset: usize) -> u64 {
    // SAFETY: Caller must ensure buffer has at least offset + 8 bytes
    unsafe { u64::from_be_bytes(*(buffer.as_ptr().add(offset) as *const [u8; 8])) }
}

impl InternalKey {
    pub(crate) fn decode(encoded_key: &[u8]) -> Self {
        let n = encoded_key.len() - 16;          // unchecked subtraction
        let trailer = read_u64_be(encoded_key, n);
        let timestamp = read_u64_be(encoded_key, n + 8);
        ...
    }
    pub(crate) fn user_key_from_encoded(encoded: &[u8]) -> &[u8] {
        &encoded[..encoded.len() - 16]           // safe slicing: panics on short input
    }
    pub(crate) fn trailer_from_encoded(encoded: &[u8]) -> u64 {
        let n = encoded.len() - 16;              // unchecked subtraction
        read_u64_be(encoded, n)
    }
    pub(crate) fn seq_num_from_encoded(encoded: &[u8]) -> u64 {
        trailer_to_seq_num(Self::trailer_from_encoded(encoded))
    }
}
```

**Arithmetic (len = slice length, base = allocation start).**

| Call | Computation | debug (overflow-checks on) | release (overflow-checks off) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `seq_num()` / `trailer()`, `len = 8` | `n = len - 16` | panic (`attempt to subtract with overflow`) | `n = usize::MAX - 7`; `base + n` wraps to `base - 8`; reads `[base-8, base)` |
| `timestamp()`, `len = 4` | `n = len - 8` | panic | `n = usize::MAX - 3`; `base + n` wraps to `base - 4`; reads `[base-4, base+4)` ← ASan-confirmed |
| `user_key()`, `len < 16` | `&encoded[..len - 16]` | panic | slice range error panic (DoS, not UB) |

`[u8; 8]` has alignment 1, so this is not an alignment issue; the SAFETY comment assumes `offset + 8` is in bounds, but the wrapped offset is a huge `usize`.

**Why safe code can reach it.** `InternalKeyRef` and `from_encoded` are `pub` at the crate root; no `unsafe` is involved at the call site; `surrealkv` is published on crates.io. Any downstream Rust program (or sibling workspace crate) can construct the reference from a short slice and call `seq_num()`/`timestamp()`/`trailer()`.

**In-tree consumer path (product impact).** `surrealkv-0.21.2/src/sstable/block.rs:684-736` (`seek_next_entry`) reconstructs `current_key` from block data with `key_end`/`value_end` upper-bound checks only - there is no `current_key.len() >= 16` check - and `:792-795` wraps it:

```rust
fn key(&self) -> InternalKeyRef<'_> {
    debug_assert!(self.valid());
    InternalKeyRef::from_encoded(&self.current_key)
}
```

The normal writer always appends the 16-byte trailer, so regular data is ≥16 bytes. A crafted or corrupted SSTable entry whose reconstructed key is 1..15 bytes therefore yields a short `InternalKeyRef`. SurrealDB's `kv-surrealkv` wrapper calls the accessors directly, e.g. (`surrealdb/core/src/kvs/surrealkv/mod.rs`):

```rust
let key_ref = iter.key();
if key_ref.timestamp() <= ts {          // line 512: touches len - 8 first
    ...
    let user_key = key_ref.user_key().to_vec();
}
```

The same shape appears at `:906-909`, `:934-938`, `:964-967`, `:992-998`, and `iter.key().user_key()` at `:865,:877,:934`. Two outcomes are possible for a short key, depending on which accessor runs first: the comparator path (`surrealkv/src/comparator.rs:146-159`, `InternalKeyComparator::compare`) calls `user_key_from_encoded` first and panics (DoS), while direct `timestamp()` callers read out of bounds. We did not construct an end-to-end crafted-SSTable reproduction (that requires a valid block checksum for a short reconstructed key); this path is reported as the missing invariant plus the consumer call shape.

**Honest scope.** The demonstrated trigger is the public safe API; no remote vector is claimed. The in-tree path requires control over / corruption of the database files. We verified only `surrealkv 0.21.2`.

**Suggested remediation.**
1. Make the precondition real: `from_encoded` returns `Option`/`Result` (or keep the `debug_assert!` and add `checked_sub`/`get` in every accessor).
2. Avoid `as_ptr().add(n)` for the typed load: use `read_unaligned` on a checked range, or copy into a `[u8; 8]`/`u64::from_be_bytes`.
3. Validate at the decode boundary: after reconstructing `current_key`, reject/handle `len < 16` in `BlockIterator::seek_next_entry` (return `SSTableError`).
4. Downstream hardening (SurrealDB): check `key_ref.encoded().len() >= 16` before calling the accessors, until an upstream fix is available.

**Disclosure.** Not disclosed publicly or to any third party. The complete PoC source and the raw `cargo test`/ASan outputs are inlined below; the full research report is available on request.

### PoC

Minimal trigger (safe code only; no `unsafe`):

```rust
use surrealkv::InternalKeyRef;

// 8-byte slice: two bytes short of the 16-byte minimum internal key
// (user_key || trailer_be(8) || timestamp_be(8)).
let v = vec![0u8; 8];
let key = InternalKeyRef::from_encoded(&v[..8]); // safe constructor; no runtime check
let seq = key.seq_num();                         // release: len - 16 underflows -> base - 8
let ts = key.timestamp();                        // release: len - 8  underflows -> base - 8
println!("{seq} {ts}");
```

Run (the PoC is an integration test; it can live in any checkout that resolves `surrealkv 0.21.2`, e.g. an existing `surrealdb/core/tests/` directory - no new directory is required there. In this repository a `tests/` directory can be created (it does not exist at `v0.21.2`); the test only needs the `surrealkv` library and no extra dependencies):

```bash
# dev profile: control + evidence that the only guard is a debug_assert
cargo test -p surrealdb-core --features kv-surrealkv \
  --test f4_surrealkv_internal_key_oob -- --nocapture

# release + ASan: the out-of-bounds read
RUSTFLAGS="-Zsanitizer=address" ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 \
  cargo +nightly-2026-04-14 test --release \
  --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -p surrealdb-core --features kv-surrealkv \
  --test f4_surrealkv_internal_key_oob -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1
```

Observed on the reporter's host (verbatim excerpts; full outputs in the collapsible blocks below):

```text
# dev
thread 'short_slice_is_rejected_only_by_debug_assert' panicked at
  .../surrealkv-0.21.2/src/lib.rs:912:3:
assertion failed: encoded.len() >= 16
test valid_encoded_key_round_trips ... ok
test short_slice_is_rejected_only_by_debug_assert - should panic ... ok
test result: ok. 2 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored

# release + ASan
==2384972==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x7744633e400c
READ of size 8 at 0x7744633e400c thread T1
    #0 ... f4_surrealkv_internal_key_oob ... +0x120140
0x7744633e400c is located 4 bytes before 4-byte region
  [0x7744633e4010,0x7744633e4014)
SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ...
==2384972==ABORTING
test result: FAILED
```

The read address is exactly `base - 4` for the 4-byte allocation, matching `timestamp()`'s `n = 4 - 8` wrap; shadow byte `fa` is the heap left redzone.

